{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>There shall not be a kedeshah among the daughters of Israel</b> — consecrated and set aside for prostitution. And there shall not be a kadesh among the sons of Israel — set aside for lying with a male, the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 23:18). Now, if this prohibition is a warning addressed to the woman who lies with a male, warning her when she is consecrated and set aside for prostitution — if so, then the unmarried woman who has intercourse casually and secretly is not among those liable for the prohibitions. And likewise the kadesh: why does the verse mention when he is a kadesh and set aside, for even one who ishidden in the innermost chambers is subject to karet and execution by the court? Furthermore, it would have been fitting to say 'There shall not be among you a kedeshah and there shall not be among you a kadesh,' for <b>from the daughters of Israel</b> and <b>from the sons of Israel</b> is as though He speaks of another nation. And what appears correct in my eyes regarding this prohibition is that it is a warning to the court, that they must not allow any one of the daughters of Israel to sit at the crossroads with eyes upon the road for promiscuity, or to set up for her a hut for prostitutes, as is the custom of foreign lands, where they sit at the doorway with timbrels and lyres, as it is written (Isaiah 23:16), 'Take a lyre, go about the city, O forgotten prostitute; play well, sing many songs, that you may be remembered.' And likewise He warns the court concerning the kadesh. And according to the plain sense, even one who lies with women in a hut that he makes for himself set aside for promiscuity, or who sits with eyes upon the road — He warns the court concerning them. And it appears to me that this is also the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 23:18), except that he joins to this the slave and the maidservant who enter Israel through marriage, because everyone knows about this slave who married a daughter of Israel that he has no binding betrothal with her, and she is with him as a wife with her husband — if so, she is a kedeshah in the eyes of the public. And I saw in the Sifrei (Sifrei Ki Teitzei 126): 'There shall not be a kedeshah from the daughters of Israel — you are not warned concerning this regarding the nations; and there shall not be a kadesh — you are not warned concerning this regarding the nations.' This is by reasoning: if regarding a kedeshah, the lighter matter, you are warned concerning Israel, then regarding a kadesh, the weightier matter, is it not logical that you should be warned concerning Israel? Or conversely: if regarding a kadesh, the weightier matter, you are not warned concerning the nations, then regarding a kedeshah, the lighter matter, is it not logical that you should not be warned concerning the nations, etc. From this it appears that their midrash is not in accordance with the words of Onkelos, for according to his view there is no greater stringency regarding the kadesh than regarding the kedeshah. Furthermore, what is the meaning of their saying 'you are not warned concerning him regarding the nations'? And likewise, if their midrash concerns the kadesh as a male who ispassive, as in the midrash of Rabbi Yishmael in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 54a), even in this case it cannot be that they would say 'you are not warned concerning him regarding the nations,' for one who israped by a Gentile is liable to stoning. Rather, it appears that the view of the author of this baraita is in accordance with the matter we have mentioned: he warns the court that the woman must not stand at the crossroads for prostitution, for there she will commit adultery with those forbidden to her both as close and distant relatives, and therefore they cover their faces to prostitute themselves even to their own brothers and relatives, as it says (Genesis 38:15), 'And he reckoned her a prostitute, for she had covered her face.' And likewise He warns the court concerning one set aside to be passively used  by males, as in the matter written (I Kings 14:24), 'And there were also kadeshim in the land; they did according to all the abominations of the nations.' And aside from the warning addressed to those who commit the transgression, He warns here concerning the court, that they must not allow a kadesh to stand with eyes upon the road, as is known of them in the land of Egypt, that they stand by the road with covered faces like women to commit this abomination. And they expounded in this baraita that we are not warned concerning others besides ourselves if they do so with one another, for we were not warned regarding the nations except in the matter of idolatry alone. And the term kadesh and kedeshah according to the opinion of the commentators is a term of designation and appointment, for they found 'I have commanded my consecrated ones' (Isaiah 13:3), and likewise 'He has consecrated His invited ones' (Zephaniah 1:7), 'Sanctify war against her' (Jeremiah 6:4). But in my opinion, all are from the root of kedushah, for one who separates from promiscuity is called kadosh, as stated (Leviticus 21:7), 'A prostitute or a profaned woman they shall not take... for he is holy to his God.' Now, she who guards herself from forbidden relations and from promiscuity is kedoshah, and she who departs from kedushah and defiles herself with promiscuity is called kedishah — analogous to what usage tends to express, 'And in all increase I would root it out' (Job 31:12), and 'They shall clear away the ashes from the altar' (Numbers 4:13), for the prostitute who publicizes herself defiles the Name greatly in the utmost confusion, separated from all kedushah. And this name applies to her only when she is set aside for this abomination, which has no time of fitness or kedushah whatsoever. And the expression <b>He has consecrated His invited ones</b> is a metaphor, for one who makes a feast consecrates his invited guests and purifies them so that they do not defile the table and the bread, as it says (I Samuel 20:26), 'He is not clean, for he is not clean' — and likewise (Jeremiah 6:4) 'Sanctify war against her' — that they should purify themselves, as one purifies his invited guests, so that the companions are not separated from them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The Hebrew text contains the transliterated Aramaic/Hebrew word נִבְעָל (literally 'isacted upon passively'), a technical halakhic term for onepassive in male same-sex relations; I have left it as 'passive' throughout to preserve the technical register without paraphrase."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:55:08.809153364Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}